1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,760
This is Doing and
Feeling, the long version.

2
00:00:03,160 --> 00:00:05,660
Episode four: Life as Computation.

3
00:00:05,940 --> 00:00:12,510
In 1948, at a symposium in Pasadena, the
mathematician John von Neumann asked a

4
00:00:12,560 --> 00:00:17,110
question that sounds like an engineering
puzzle and turns out to be a question about

5
00:00:17,160 --> 00:00:20,060
life. Could a machine
build a copy of itself?

6
00:00:20,300 --> 00:00:22,260
The difficulty is a regress.

7
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:25,760
To build a copy, the machine
needs a description of itself.

8
00:00:25,980 --> 00:00:30,410
But the description is part of the machine,
so it must contain a description of the

9
00:00:30,460 --> 00:00:32,340
description, and so on forever.

10
00:00:32,960 --> 00:00:36,740
Von Neumann's way out was to use one
description in two different ways.

11
00:00:37,040 --> 00:00:40,820
A constructor reads the description
and builds what it specifies.

12
00:00:41,060 --> 00:00:45,080
A copier duplicates the description
blindly, without interpreting it.

13
00:00:45,600 --> 00:00:49,810
Build the offspring from the instructions,
copy the instructions without reading

14
00:00:49,860 --> 00:00:53,100
them, hand the copy over,
and the regress disappears.

15
00:00:53,620 --> 00:00:58,570
And if the copying is occasionally imperfect,
the offspring differ, which supplies

16
00:00:58,620 --> 00:01:00,340
the variation evolution needs.

17
00:01:00,560 --> 00:01:04,520
This was five years before Watson
and Crick described the double helix.

18
00:01:04,780 --> 00:01:07,440
Yet it closely parallels
how cells use DNA.

19
00:01:07,980 --> 00:01:13,750
Genes are read, transcribed and translated
to build proteins, and the same DNA is

20
00:01:13,800 --> 00:01:17,780
copied, base by base, without being
interpreted, to make a new cell.

21
00:01:18,580 --> 00:01:22,640
Von Neumann had worked out the logical
architecture of life from first principles.

22
00:01:23,740 --> 00:01:28,380
This episode makes the strongest case
I can for one claim, and then tests it.

23
00:01:28,560 --> 00:01:33,180
The claim is that life, intelligence
and computation are a single continuum.

24
00:01:33,480 --> 00:01:39,350
It has four steps. First, replicators
arise naturally wherever computation and

25
00:01:39,400 --> 00:01:43,900
interaction meet. Second, the big
leaps in complexity come from merging.

26
00:01:44,340 --> 00:01:49,230
Third, anything that keeps itself going
must regulate, and good regulation requires

27
00:01:49,280 --> 00:01:54,020
a model of what is being regulated, which
makes prediction the core of being alive.

28
00:01:54,700 --> 00:01:59,550
Fourth, the hardest things to predict
are other predictors, so minds grew by

29
00:01:59,600 --> 00:02:04,490
modeling other minds. The most ambitious
recent statement of this view is a 2025

30
00:02:04,540 --> 00:02:10,490
book by Blaise Agüera y Arcas, published
by MIT Press and the Antikythera project

31
00:02:10,540 --> 00:02:12,240
and freely readable online.

32
00:02:12,640 --> 00:02:15,420
Its title is a question:
What Is Intelligence?

33
00:02:15,840 --> 00:02:17,800
I'll use his argument as a spine.

34
00:02:18,080 --> 00:02:21,060
The test comes at the end,
and it is a serious one:

35
00:02:21,260 --> 00:02:24,240
if everything computes, does
the claim say anything at all?

36
00:02:25,340 --> 00:02:30,670
Start with replicators. Von Neumann went
on to design a self-reproducing pattern in

37
00:02:30,720 --> 00:02:36,530
a cellular automaton, a grid of cells, each
in one of twenty-nine states, updated by

38
00:02:36,580 --> 00:02:40,640
local rules. It was published
after his death, in 1966.

39
00:02:41,180 --> 00:02:46,610
In 1970, John Conway's Game of Life showed
that two states and a handful of rules

40
00:02:46,660 --> 00:02:51,600
could support gliders, oscillators
and, eventually, universal computation.

41
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:58,030
In 1984, Christopher Langton found a far
simpler self-replicating pattern, now

42
00:02:58,080 --> 00:03:02,000
called Langton's loops. Then
came artificial life in software.

43
00:03:02,240 --> 00:03:08,670
In 1991, the ecologist Thomas Ray released
Tierra, a virtual computer filled with

44
00:03:08,720 --> 00:03:11,740
self-copying programs
competing for processor time.

45
00:03:12,240 --> 00:03:17,170
Parasites evolved that borrowed other
programs' copying code, and hosts evolved

46
00:03:17,220 --> 00:03:23,690
defenses. In 2003, Richard Lenski, Charles
Ofria and colleagues used a platform

47
00:03:23,740 --> 00:03:28,610
called Avida to show how a complex logical
function could evolve through a series of

48
00:03:28,660 --> 00:03:31,900
simpler steps, some of them
harmful when they first arose.

49
00:03:32,160 --> 00:03:38,150
So the idea is old. But in Tierra, the
first replicator was written by hand, and

50
00:03:38,200 --> 00:03:40,960
Avida rewards programs
for doing useful logic.

51
00:03:41,280 --> 00:03:46,520
That's what makes a 2024 experiment by
Agüera y Arcas and colleagues at Google,

52
00:03:46,800 --> 00:03:50,640
including Ben Laurie and Alexander
Mordvintsev, so striking.

53
00:03:51,020 --> 00:03:55,440
They built a primordial soup of about a
hundred and thirty thousand random programs,

54
00:03:55,920 --> 00:04:00,410
each sixty-four bytes long, written in a
variant of a famously minimal programming

55
00:04:00,460 --> 00:04:02,600
language whose name is unprintable.

56
00:04:02,760 --> 00:04:04,460
They called the variant BFF.

57
00:04:04,900 --> 00:04:09,190
Code and data lived on the same tape,
so programs could read and overwrite

58
00:04:09,240 --> 00:04:10,400
themselves and each other.

59
00:04:10,600 --> 00:04:15,610
The procedure was simple. Pick two programs
at random, glue them together, run the

60
00:04:15,660 --> 00:04:19,140
result, split it back into two
halves, and return them to the soup.

61
00:04:19,400 --> 00:04:24,110
Repeat millions of times. There was
no fitness function, no reward and no

62
00:04:24,160 --> 00:04:27,860
hand-written ancestor. For a
long time, nothing much happened.

63
00:04:28,240 --> 00:04:31,950
Then, in about forty percent of
runs, there was a sudden transition.

64
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:37,290
Self-replicating programs appeared, spread
and took over the soup, and the measured

65
00:04:37,340 --> 00:04:39,200
complexity of the population jumped.

66
00:04:39,660 --> 00:04:43,850
It happened even with random mutation
switched off, so the replicators were

67
00:04:43,900 --> 00:04:48,690
assembled through interaction and
self-modification rather than found by luck in the

68
00:04:48,740 --> 00:04:53,460
initial noise. Surely the instruction
set is doing a lot of the work.

69
00:04:53,720 --> 00:04:56,100
The authors tested exactly that.

70
00:04:56,620 --> 00:05:01,010
Replicators also emerged in versions
built on the Forth language and on the

71
00:05:01,060 --> 00:05:04,120
instruction set of the old
Z-eighty microprocessor.

72
00:05:04,400 --> 00:05:08,950
In a minimal language called SUBLEQ,
they never arose on their own, though

73
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:11,300
replicators planted by hand could spread.

74
00:05:11,760 --> 00:05:16,310
So the substrate matters. What the
experiment shows is that under fairly generic

75
00:05:16,360 --> 00:05:21,980
conditions, replication can arise without
design and, once it arises, dominates.

76
00:05:22,260 --> 00:05:26,970
What it does not show is how chemistry
did it, or that the soup goes on evolving

77
00:05:27,020 --> 00:05:32,770
open-ended novelty. Agüera y Arcas's
reading is that life is an attractor that

78
00:05:32,820 --> 00:05:36,920
computation falls into.
The second step is merging.

79
00:05:37,340 --> 00:05:42,730
In the classic picture, complexity grows
by small mutations filtered by competition.

80
00:05:42,780 --> 00:05:49,230
Agüera y Arcas argues that the biggest
leaps came from symbiogenesis, the fusion of

81
00:05:49,280 --> 00:05:50,820
once-independent organisms.

82
00:05:51,340 --> 00:05:57,630
Around 1905 to 1910, the Russian botanist
Konstantin Mereschkowski proposed that

83
00:05:57,680 --> 00:06:02,930
chloroplasts, the photosynthetic machinery
in plant cells, were once free-living

84
00:06:02,980 --> 00:06:08,750
bacteria. The idea was largely ignored
until Lynn Margulis revived and extended it

85
00:06:08,800 --> 00:06:14,770
in a 1967 paper, published under the name
Lynn Sagan and reportedly rejected by

86
00:06:14,820 --> 00:06:16,260
about fifteen journals first.

87
00:06:16,800 --> 00:06:18,620
Today it's textbook biology.

88
00:06:19,040 --> 00:06:23,890
The mitochondria in your cells descend
from bacteria engulfed by an ancestral cell

89
00:06:23,940 --> 00:06:28,340
roughly two billion years ago, and they
still carry small genomes of their own.

90
00:06:28,520 --> 00:06:34,910
In 1995, John Maynard Smith and Eörs
Szathmáry generalized the pattern in The Major

91
00:06:34,960 --> 00:06:40,450
Transitions in Evolution: replicating
molecules joining into chromosomes, simple

92
00:06:40,500 --> 00:06:46,330
cells merging into complex ones, single
cells forming multicellular bodies, solitary

93
00:06:46,380 --> 00:06:50,880
animals forming colonies, and human
societies held together by language.

94
00:06:51,060 --> 00:06:56,130
In each transition, entities that once
reproduced independently come to reproduce

95
00:06:56,180 --> 00:06:57,970
only as parts of a larger whole.

96
00:06:58,020 --> 00:07:04,150
Agüera y Arcas reads the BFF soup the
same way, with replicators assembled from

97
00:07:04,200 --> 00:07:08,550
cooperating fragments, and he argues
that humans and machine intelligence are

98
00:07:08,600 --> 00:07:10,320
entering such a transition now.

99
00:07:10,680 --> 00:07:15,100
That last claim is a prediction, and
we'll meet a rival reading of it shortly.

100
00:07:16,200 --> 00:07:20,690
The third step is the one that turns
life into prediction, and it runs through

101
00:07:20,740 --> 00:07:26,470
physics. In 1943, Erwin Schrödinger lectured
in Dublin on a question he published as

102
00:07:26,520 --> 00:07:30,390
What Is Life? How do living things
stay ordered against the second law of

103
00:07:30,440 --> 00:07:36,290
thermodynamics? They feed, he said, on
negative entropy, maintaining internal order

104
00:07:36,340 --> 00:07:38,420
by exporting disorder
to their surroundings.

105
00:07:39,010 --> 00:07:43,870
He also guessed that heredity must be
stored in an aperiodic crystal, orderly but

106
00:07:43,920 --> 00:07:47,920
not repetitive, an idea that
inspired both Crick and Watson.

107
00:07:48,280 --> 00:07:50,680
Here's the bridge I find most compelling.

108
00:07:50,940 --> 00:07:56,410
In 2012, Susanne Still and colleagues
proved a theorem about a broad class of

109
00:07:56,460 --> 00:07:59,390
systems driven by a
fluctuating environment.

110
00:07:59,440 --> 00:08:03,560
Such a system inevitably retains
information about the past.

111
00:08:03,880 --> 00:08:06,640
Some of that information
helps predict the future.

112
00:08:06,860 --> 00:08:11,910
The rest, which they called nostalgia, is
useless for prediction, and the energy the

113
00:08:11,960 --> 00:08:15,820
system wastes as heat is proportional
to how much of it the system keeps.

114
00:08:16,340 --> 00:08:20,960
In other words, a memory that doesn't
predict is thermodynamically wasteful.

115
00:08:21,420 --> 00:08:26,420
Efficiency and prediction are the same
pressure, described in two vocabularies.

116
00:08:26,620 --> 00:08:28,870
Which ties back to episode three.

117
00:08:28,920 --> 00:08:33,850
If good prediction is good compression,
then life is under physical pressure to

118
00:08:33,900 --> 00:08:39,230
compress its world. And that's exactly
where one recent attempt to define life has

119
00:08:39,280 --> 00:08:45,550
run into trouble. In a 2023 paper in Nature,
Lee Cronin, Sara Walker and colleagues

120
00:08:45,600 --> 00:08:50,090
argued for assembly theory, which scores
an object by the minimum number of joining

121
00:08:50,140 --> 00:08:54,970
steps needed to build it, and treats
complex objects found in many copies as a

122
00:08:55,020 --> 00:08:56,710
signature of selection and life.

123
00:08:56,760 --> 00:09:01,730
Critics led by Hector Zenil replied that
the measure is essentially a weak form of

124
00:09:01,780 --> 00:09:06,230
compression, a cousin of the algorithms
behind zip files, and that it neither

125
00:09:06,280 --> 00:09:08,340
explains nor quantifies selection.

126
00:09:08,860 --> 00:09:13,260
The assembly theorists reject that
comparison, and the argument continues.

127
00:09:13,740 --> 00:09:18,490
Either way, it shows how quickly attempts
to define life end up in the information

128
00:09:18,540 --> 00:09:22,400
theory of episode three. One more
definition matters for later.

129
00:09:22,660 --> 00:09:28,110
In the early nineteen seventies, the
Chilean biologists Humberto Maturana and

130
00:09:28,160 --> 00:09:33,630
Francisco Varela defined a living
system as autopoietic, self-producing:

131
00:09:33,680 --> 00:09:38,320
a network of processes that continually
makes the very components that make it up.

132
00:09:38,540 --> 00:09:40,400
Notice the difference from von Neumann.

133
00:09:40,660 --> 00:09:44,000
His replicator is defined
by a description it copies.

134
00:09:44,960 --> 00:09:48,520
Autopoiesis is defined by a body
that keeps rebuilding itself.

135
00:09:48,940 --> 00:09:54,070
That difference will become the center of
the skeptics' case in episode thirteen.

136
00:09:54,120 --> 00:10:00,970
Now regulation. In 1943, Arturo Rosenblueth,
Norbert Wiener and Julian Bigelow

137
00:10:01,020 --> 00:10:04,920
published a short paper called
Behavior, Purpose and Teleology.

138
00:10:05,540 --> 00:10:09,870
Purposeful behavior, they proposed, is
simply behavior controlled by negative

139
00:10:09,920 --> 00:10:14,890
feedback. A thermostat that turns on the
heat when the room gets cold is, in this

140
00:10:14,940 --> 00:10:16,820
minimal sense, pursuing a goal.

141
00:10:17,340 --> 00:10:20,500
Wiener named the field
cybernetics in 1948.

142
00:10:21,360 --> 00:10:24,660
W. Ross Ashby added the
law of requisite variety:

143
00:10:24,920 --> 00:10:29,910
a regulator must be able to produce at
least as much variety as the disturbances it

144
00:10:29,960 --> 00:10:35,350
is trying to cancel. In 1970, Roger Conant
and Ashby published a theorem with a

145
00:10:35,400 --> 00:10:39,860
wonderful title: every good regulator of
a system must be a model of that system.

146
00:10:40,080 --> 00:10:42,880
The careful statement is
narrower than the slogan.

147
00:10:43,160 --> 00:10:48,010
They showed that the simplest regulator
achieving optimal results must map the

148
00:10:48,060 --> 00:10:51,140
system's states onto its
own, in effect mirroring it.

149
00:10:51,460 --> 00:10:56,590
The slogan, that every controller secretly
contains a world model, goes further than

150
00:10:56,640 --> 00:11:01,480
the proof. That's one justification
for model-based reinforcement learning.

151
00:11:01,620 --> 00:11:06,570
If you want to control an environment
well, learn a model of how it responds.

152
00:11:06,620 --> 00:11:13,380
And in 2025, Jonathan Richens and colleagues
gave the idea a modern, precise form.

153
00:11:13,640 --> 00:11:17,990
They proved that any agent able to
generalize across a wide enough range of

154
00:11:18,040 --> 00:11:22,650
multi-step, goal-directed tasks must
have learned a predictive model of its

155
00:11:22,700 --> 00:11:27,530
environment, one that can in principle be
extracted from its behavior, and that more

156
00:11:27,580 --> 00:11:30,300
capable agents require
more accurate models.

157
00:11:30,540 --> 00:11:33,960
That's the bridge Agüera
y Arcas's argument needs.

158
00:11:34,660 --> 00:11:40,150
Survival requires regulation, flexible
regulation requires a model, and a model that

159
00:11:40,200 --> 00:11:42,340
runs ahead of the present is a predictor.

160
00:11:43,180 --> 00:11:46,400
The fourth step:
intelligence becomes social.

161
00:11:46,860 --> 00:11:51,470
Much of any animal's environment consists
of other animals, and the hardest things

162
00:11:51,520 --> 00:11:53,820
in the world to predict
are other predictors.

163
00:11:54,060 --> 00:11:59,510
In 1976, the psychologist Nicholas
Humphrey published The Social Function of

164
00:11:59,560 --> 00:12:04,510
Intellect. His argument was that for many
primates, the practical problems of daily

165
00:12:04,560 --> 00:12:07,760
life are not that demanding,
but social life is:

166
00:12:08,380 --> 00:12:12,560
alliances, deception, reciprocity,
remembering who did what to whom.

167
00:12:12,920 --> 00:12:17,680
Primate intelligence, he proposed,
evolved mainly to handle other primates.

168
00:12:18,180 --> 00:12:22,490
Richard Byrne and Andrew Whiten developed
this into the Machiavellian intelligence

169
00:12:22,540 --> 00:12:28,570
hypothesis, and Robin Dunbar found that
across primate species, the relative size of

170
00:12:28,620 --> 00:12:31,600
the neocortex tracks
typical social group size.

171
00:12:32,040 --> 00:12:36,830
Extrapolated to humans, it suggests a
natural group of about a hundred and fifty,

172
00:12:36,880 --> 00:12:38,380
now called Dunbar's number.

173
00:12:38,640 --> 00:12:41,060
The correlation has been challenged too.

174
00:12:41,300 --> 00:12:46,870
A 2017 study of more than a hundred and
forty primate species found that diet

175
00:12:46,920 --> 00:12:49,710
predicted brain size
better than group size did.

176
00:12:49,760 --> 00:12:52,780
Agüera y Arcas turns this into an engine.

177
00:12:52,960 --> 00:12:58,060
If I predict you and you predict me, each
of us gains by modeling the other's model,

178
00:12:58,340 --> 00:13:00,000
including the other's model of us.

179
00:13:00,080 --> 00:13:04,310
That recursive mutual modeling is an
arms race with no natural ceiling.

180
00:13:04,360 --> 00:13:09,170
And once a brain has machinery for modeling
other minds, it can turn that machinery

181
00:13:09,220 --> 00:13:14,460
on itself. Self-awareness, on this
view, is theory of mind pointed inward.

182
00:13:14,920 --> 00:13:17,220
The idea has a surprising ancestor.

183
00:13:17,480 --> 00:13:23,190
In a section added to The Gay Science
in 1887, Friedrich Nietzsche argued that

184
00:13:23,240 --> 00:13:27,510
consciousness developed only under the
pressure of the need to communicate, which is

185
00:13:27,560 --> 00:13:31,280
why, he said, it is the most
superficial part of the mind.

186
00:13:31,560 --> 00:13:36,050
You don't need his contempt to see the
outline of modern theories, including the

187
00:13:36,100 --> 00:13:39,590
attention schema theory we'll
examine in episode eight.

188
00:13:39,640 --> 00:13:45,500
Agüera y Arcas carries the continuum all
the way to consciousness and free will,

189
00:13:45,720 --> 00:13:50,370
through a relational, social account,
though his confidence is greatest about

190
00:13:50,420 --> 00:13:55,170
intelligence. We'll weigh his view of
consciousness in episode eight, and the fourth

191
00:13:55,220 --> 00:13:57,280
test below targets that last step.

192
00:13:58,280 --> 00:14:01,920
Now the test, because a theory
this sweeping deserves pressure.

193
00:14:02,260 --> 00:14:04,840
The first objection is
pancomputationalism.

194
00:14:05,020 --> 00:14:11,090
In 1988, Hilary Putnam argued that, under
a suitably contrived mapping, any ordinary

195
00:14:11,140 --> 00:14:14,340
physical system implements any
finite-state automaton you like.

196
00:14:14,760 --> 00:14:16,480
John Searle put it more memorably:

197
00:14:16,720 --> 00:14:21,050
the wall behind him, he said, could be
described as running the WordStar word

198
00:14:21,100 --> 00:14:26,310
processor. If computation is that cheap,
then life is computation and minds are

199
00:14:26,360 --> 00:14:29,120
computation become trivially
true and say nothing.

200
00:14:29,460 --> 00:14:31,460
That always struck me as a trick.

201
00:14:31,780 --> 00:14:36,480
The mapping is invented after the fact,
state by state, and it predicts nothing.

202
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:39,580
That's essentially David Chalmers's reply.

203
00:14:39,860 --> 00:14:45,570
In 1996 he published a paper asking whether
a rock implements every finite-state

204
00:14:45,620 --> 00:14:51,250
automaton. Real implementation, he
argued, requires the right counterfactual

205
00:14:51,300 --> 00:14:55,370
structure. The physical system must be
disposed to follow the computation's

206
00:14:55,420 --> 00:15:00,690
transitions, including in situations that
never actually occur, and it must respond

207
00:15:00,740 --> 00:15:04,970
to inputs. A wall has no such
dispositions for WordStar.

208
00:15:05,020 --> 00:15:10,140
On that stricter notion, saying a cell
computes is a substantive and testable claim,

209
00:15:10,300 --> 00:15:12,880
and a cell copying its DNA passes it.

210
00:15:13,320 --> 00:15:16,840
The wall does not. The second
objection is about purpose.

211
00:15:17,180 --> 00:15:21,710
The cybernetic slogan that the purpose
of a system is what it does makes every

212
00:15:21,760 --> 00:15:25,940
effect a purpose, so the purpose of the
heart would include making thumping noises.

213
00:15:26,780 --> 00:15:31,650
Philosophers of biology like Ruth Millikan
distinguish an effect from a function, an

214
00:15:31,700 --> 00:15:34,140
effect that explains why
the trait was selected.

215
00:15:34,340 --> 00:15:37,480
The evolutionary story can
supply that distinction.

216
00:15:37,740 --> 00:15:42,990
The slogan alone cannot. The third objection
targets the claim that language models

217
00:15:43,040 --> 00:15:46,150
mark a new major
transition in intelligence.

218
00:15:46,200 --> 00:15:52,850
In 2025, Henry Farrell, Alison Gopnik,
Cosma Shalizi and James Evans argued

219
00:15:52,900 --> 00:15:56,750
in Science that large models are better
understood as cultural and social

220
00:15:56,800 --> 00:16:01,180
technologies, like writing,
print, markets and bureaucracies.

221
00:16:01,420 --> 00:16:06,890
On their view, these models are powerful
new ways for humans to store, access and

222
00:16:06,940 --> 00:16:11,270
recombine what other humans have produced,
rather than new agents in their own

223
00:16:11,320 --> 00:16:16,430
right. That reading can grant that these
systems are superb predictors while denying

224
00:16:16,480 --> 00:16:18,180
that a new kind of mind has appeared.

225
00:16:18,380 --> 00:16:22,040
And the fourth objection is the one
that matters most for this series:

226
00:16:22,460 --> 00:16:24,880
the slide from intelligence
to consciousness.

227
00:16:25,300 --> 00:16:30,050
The first steps of the argument, that life
involves computation, that intelligence

228
00:16:30,100 --> 00:16:35,250
is largely prediction, and that minds
were shaped by social modeling, all have

229
00:16:35,300 --> 00:16:38,040
serious defenders,
though each is contested.

230
00:16:38,160 --> 00:16:42,450
The final step, that a system with the
right predictive and social modeling thereby

231
00:16:42,500 --> 00:16:47,830
has experience, is exactly what biological
naturalists and integrated information

232
00:16:47,880 --> 00:16:51,690
theory deny, as we'll see in
episodes nine and thirteen.

233
00:16:51,740 --> 00:16:57,700
Agüera y Arcas's relational view is a
coherent answer, but it's one that a dualist,

234
00:16:57,960 --> 00:17:01,060
a panpsychist or Anil Seth
would reject at the root.

235
00:17:01,960 --> 00:17:05,220
So how much of the continuum
survives the tests?

236
00:17:05,600 --> 00:17:07,560
Most of the first three steps.

237
00:17:07,760 --> 00:17:10,940
The computational view of
life is more than a metaphor:

238
00:17:11,740 --> 00:17:16,750
von Neumann's architecture closely parallels
how cells reproduce, and the BFF soup

239
00:17:16,800 --> 00:17:21,820
shows that replication can emerge from
computation plus interaction without design.

240
00:17:22,140 --> 00:17:27,810
Regulation requires modeling, and physics
penalizes memories that don't predict, so

241
00:17:27,860 --> 00:17:31,060
prediction sits at the heart of
anything that keeps itself going.

242
00:17:31,380 --> 00:17:36,530
And the hardest prediction problem many
animals face is other animals, which may be

243
00:17:36,580 --> 00:17:40,060
why intelligence, and perhaps
the self, has social roots.

244
00:17:40,340 --> 00:17:44,950
What the view doesn't settle, and can't
settle on its own terms, is whether any of

245
00:17:45,000 --> 00:17:48,340
this computation is felt. That's
for the rest of the series.

246
00:17:48,740 --> 00:17:52,240
First, we follow prediction into
the organ that does it best.

247
00:17:52,600 --> 00:17:54,940
Next episode: the predictive brain.
